    <!-- ==========================================================================================
         CARD 1 : Temperatura CPU
         ========================================================================================== -->

    <element name="temp_hdr_icon" type="image" xpos="262" ypos="130" width="48" height="48">icons/card_thermo.svg</element>
    <element name="temp_hdr_text" type="text"  class="card-title" xpos="320" ypos="114" sizefont="19">Temperatura CPU</element>

    <!-- thermo_empty.png/thermo_full.png are the "glass tube" artwork supplied by the designer, with the
         0/20/40/60/80/100 numeric scale and the "°C" unit baked directly into the bitmap (unlike the earlier
         flat icons, which needed separate temp_scale_* text elements to show a scale at all, now removed).
         Draw_ProgressImage draws these at their native pixel size (PutBitmapAlpha), so the on-screen size comes
         from the PNG's own dimensions (180x270, scaled up from the mockup comparison) rather than from
         width/height here; those two attributes are kept in sync with the asset purely for layout/hit-region
         bookkeeping. There is still no CSS color/tint hook for this widget type, so the tube's color stays
         whatever the bitmap contains.
         offsetstart/offsetend were measured directly against the 180x270 asset, not guessed proportionally:
         the tube's own red fill (in thermo_full.png) starts at pixel row 25, right where the rounded top cap
         reaches its full width, and the bulb's fixed red disc (identical in both images, so it does not need
         "revealing" at all) begins at row 193; hence offsetend=25 (top margin) and offsetstart=270-193=77
         (bottom margin).

         Column layout (xpos=262/466 below): the card's own left inner margin is 24 (card left=238, and
         temp_hdr_icon already sits at x=262 above), so the tube's left edge was moved to xpos=262 to reuse
         that exact same margin and to line up under the header icon. The right-hand group (cpu_temp_value,
         temp_cap_icon, temp_cap_text) is placed at xpos=466, i.e. the same 24-unit gap again measured from the
         tube's own right edge (262+180+24=466), so the left margin of the tube and the gap between the tube
         and the right-hand group are equal, as requested. All three right-hand elements share that same
         xpos=466 so they line up vertically with each other; 466+142=608 stays inside the card's right edge
         (614) even for the widest live value, "100°C" at sizefont 50 (measured against Nunito-SemiBold.ttf).

         Vertical position (ypos below) was re-measured against Abraham's clean reference crop of the target
         card: mapping that reference's own card rectangle onto this card's coordinates (both are ~376x390),
         the tube's bottom edge sits at ~87% of the card's height and the value/icon/caption group sits noticeably
         higher/tighter than our previous ypos values, so all four were raised: cpu_temp_bar ypos 416->405 (kept
         clear of temp_hdr_icon, whose bottom edge is at y=130, by 5 units), cpu_temp_value 276->263, temp_cap_icon
         377->364, temp_cap_text 402->389; a uniform ~13-unit rise that preserves the spacing already measured
         between them (it matched the reference closely) while moving the whole block up to match. The reference's
         tube is proportionally narrower than this 180-wide asset (drawn at native pixel size, so width/height
         above cannot itself compensate); xpos=262/466 already matches the reference's horizontal fractions
         closely (measured within ~1%), so only the vertical position needed correcting here. -->
    <element name="cpu_temp_bar" type="progressimage" xpos="262" ypos="405" width="180" height="270"
             direction="vertical" allocationtext="none"
             offsetstart="77" offsetend="25" level="0"
             imageempty="thermo_empty.png" imagefull="thermo_full.png">
    </element>

    <element name="cpu_temp_value" type="text" class="value-temp" xpos="466" ypos="263" sizefont="50">#[CPU_TEMPERATURE]</element>

    <!-- Mockup's degree unit is a visibly smaller glyph than the number, baseline-aligned with it (same
         "big number + small unit" pattern also used for the ring "%" and the RAM "GB"; this is the
         first of those to get it, being the simplest case: a single, left-aligned value, no centering to
         re-derive. #[CPU_TEMPERATURE] itself no longer carries "°C" (see HardwareInfo_UpdateCPU's updated
         doc comment in UI_System.cpp): it is now digits-only, so this static text supplies the unit.
         xpos=532 is a FIXED offset tuned for the common 2-digit case (466 + 60px, "62" at sizefont 50,
         Nunito-SemiBold, + a 6px gap): GEN positions left-aligned text at a fixed xpos regardless of the
         mask's rendered width, so there is no built-in way to make this element "flow" after a sibling of
         varying width without new plumbing (an app-level text-width query GEN doesn't expose today). A
         1-digit reading ("5°C") will show a slightly wider gap than a 3-digit one ("100°C") a slightly
         tighter one; flagged to Abraham as a known, deliberate trade-off rather than silently accepted. -->
    <element name="cpu_temp_unit" type="text" class="value-temp" xpos="532" ypos="263" sizefont="28">°C</element>

    <!-- Mockup swaps the small caption icon for a red CPU chip instead of a second (redundant) thermometer
         glyph: cap_cpu.svg is card_cpu.svg's same chip artwork recoloured to the theme's accent-red
         (248,81,73), matching cpu_temp_value's colour and cap_thermo.svg's old red tone exactly. -->
    <element name="temp_cap_icon" type="image" xpos="466" ypos="364" width="60" height="60">icons/cap_cpu.svg</element>
    <!-- xpos=470, not 466: measured against Abraham's own build screenshot that cpu_temp_value/temp_cap_icon's
         glyphs render ~4px right of their declared xpos=466 (Nunito-SemiBold's left-side bearing at sizefont=50,
         and cap_cpu.svg's own inner margin, both push their visible edge in a bit), while this 14pt label has
         almost none; matching the RENDERED edge (not just the declared xpos) is what makes the three look
         aligned on screen. -->
    <element name="temp_cap_text" type="text"  class="label"      xpos="470" ypos="389" sizefont="14">Temperatura actual</element>


    <!-- ==========================================================================================
         CARD 2 : Uso actual de CPU
         ========================================================================================== -->

    <element name="cpu_hdr_icon" type="image" xpos="660" ypos="130" width="48" height="48">icons/card_cpu.svg</element>
    <element name="cpu_hdr_text" type="text"  class="card-title" xpos="718" ypos="114" sizefont="19">Uso actual de CPU</element>

    <!-- Measured against Abraham's mockup (comparing the CPU-usage card's own rectangle to the ring's): the
         ring there fills roughly 69% of the card's width, well past this ring's previous 200/376=53%. Grown to
         260 (69%), re-centred (694 = 636 + (376-260)/2, same centre x=824 the caption below was already tuned
         for) and thickness/value font scaled by the same ~1.3 factor so the stroke and the "37%" text keep
         their prior visual proportion to the ring's new size. -->
    <element name="cpu_usage_radial" type="progressradial" class="ring-cpu"
             xpos="694" ypos="412" width="260" height="260"
             level="0" thickness="28" roundcap="yes" startangle="-90" sweepangle="360"
             gradientcolor="56,139,253" gradientmode="track">
      <!-- maxsizetext="4" reserves the box width of the widest possible value ("100%", 4 chars) instead of the
           CURRENT value's width. Without it, CalculateBoundaryLine_Text() auto-sizes this text element's own
           box to the exact pixel width of "42%"/"7%"/"100%" as the level changes; since that box is what gets
           re-centered inside the ring on every update, a narrower-to-wider (or wider-to-narrower) value change
           visibly resizes and re-shifts the box for a frame, reading as a jump/flash. Fixing the box at the
           4-char reservation makes the width constant across every level, so only the CSS text-align:center
           offset (see .ring-value in dashboard.css) has to redo its (now real) job of centering the actual
           string inside that fixed box, with no size change and so no visible jump. -->
      <element name="cpu_usage_value" type="text" class="ring-value" xpos="center" ypos="center" sizefont="58" maxsizetext="4">#[PROGRESSBAR_PERCENT]</element>
    </element>

    <element name="cpu_usage_caption" type="text" class="label-accent" xpos="786" ypos="438" sizefont="15">Uso actual</element>


